          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes affects blood sugar levels primarily due to insulin resistance. In this condition, the body's tissues do not respond effectively to insulin, a hormone that helps regulate glucose (sugar) in the blood. As a result, the pancreas tries to produce more insulin to overcome this resistance, but it can't always meet the increased demand. This leads to higher blood glucose levels that remain above the normal range. \n\nThe condition typically develops over time, and before it becomes diagnosed as type 2 diabetes, individuals may experience a phase known as prediabetes, where blood sugar levels are elevated but not yet in the diabetic range. This gradual process is important to recognize because early intervention through lifestyle changes can have significant effects in managing blood sugar levels (source: General clinical reference).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
